  "tour": {
    "title": "Hesed: More Than a Single English Word",
    "intro": "This tour starts with the translation problem, then lets hesed take shape in lived stories. You’ll hear it as loyal, generous action within a lasting relationship—not merely a warm feeling, and not merely a synonym for kindness. Ruth and David make the definition concrete from two different angles.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:loyal-love-god",
        "startSec": 1659,
        "endSec": 1752,
        "why": "Begin here for the core definition. The speakers connect Micah’s command to “love chesed” with “desire, loyal love,” then make the crucial distinction that “Chesed is not a feeling” but “a visible action that shows your posture in the relationship.”",
        "episodeTitle": "The Loyal Love of God",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f",
        "durationSec": 4105,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:a88b1fb7-fd1c-415f-b24c-98322c767992",
        "startSec": 948,
        "endSec": 1075,
        "why": "This gives the word a human face in Ruth. The teachers explain why translations range from “covenant faithfulness” and “loyalty” to “steadfast love,” then show Naomi blessing Ruth and Ruth responding with a costly, enduring commitment. It demonstrates that hesed is concrete loyalty, especially when there is little or nothing to gain in return.",
        "episodeTitle": "Ruth 1-2: God Fills What's Empty",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG9761838334.mp3?updated=1664486106",
        "durationSec": 3645,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:478e6896-2d24-4806-ac5f-4db234766229",
        "startSec": 1460,
        "endSec": 1690,
        "why": "Finish with David’s treatment of Mephibosheth, where hesed is explicitly called “the kindness of God.” The discussion shows David choosing to extend covenant loyalty to Jonathan’s vulnerable son, helping you see how hesed can be generous, surprising, and enacted by one person on another’s behalf.",
        "episodeTitle": "2 Samuel 8:9-9:13 (Episode 267)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_ep_667.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2471,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "After these clips, a useful working gloss is “loyal love expressed in concrete, generous action,” especially within a bond or commitment. No single English word captures every use, so keep the range—steadfast love, covenant faithfulness, mercy, kindness—in view, while remembering that the stories show what the word does.",
    "totalSeconds": 450
